<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Piet Heyn</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1577–1629</span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as Piet Hein</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Piet Heyn was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> naval officer and privateer in the service of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-west-india-company">Dutch West India Company</a>. In 1628 he trapped the Spanish treasure fleet in the Bay of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/matanzas">Matanzas</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a> and captured its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a>; the booty financed the company's conquests in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a> and paid its shareholders a large dividend. Appointed lieutenant-admiral of Holland, he was killed fighting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dunkirk">Dunkirk</a> privateers in 1629.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/piet-heyn.jpg" alt="Piet Heyn" width="320" height="421" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: After Jan Daemen Cool · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Piet_Hein_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
